      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, we need to find the number of positive integer solutions to the equation:\n\n\\[\nn \\cdot m = 86400\n\\]\n\nHere, \\( n \\) represents the number of periods, and \\( m \\) represents the duration of each period in seconds.\n\nFirst, we need to find all the pairs \\((n, m)\\) that satisfy this equation. This involves finding the factors of 86400 and then determining how many factors there are.\n\n### Step 1: Prime Factorization of 86400\nWe start by finding the prime factorization of 86400. We divide by the smallest prime numbers until we are left with only prime factors.\n\n\\[\n86400 \\div 2 = 43200\n\\]\n\\[\n43200 \\div 2 = 21600\n\\]\n\\[\n21600 \\div 2 = 10800\n\\]\n\\[\n10800 \\div 2 = 5400\n\\]\n\\[\n5400 \\div 2 = 2700\n\\]\n\\[\n2700 \\div 2 = 1350\n\\]\n\\[\n1350 \\div 2 = 675\n\\]\n\\[\n675 \\div 3 = 225\n\\]\n\\[\n225 \\div 3 = 75\n\\]\n\\[\n75 \\div 3 = 25\n\\]\n\\[\n25 \\div 5 = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n5 \\div 5 = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the prime factorization of 86400 is:\n\n\\[\n86400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Number of Factors\nTo find the total number of positive integer pairs \\((n, m)\\) that satisfy \\( n \\cdot m = 86400 \\), we use the formula for the number of factors of a number. If a number \\( N \\) has a prime factorization:\n\n\\[\nN = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k}\n\\]\n\nthen the number of factors of \\( N \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n(e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\n\\]\n\nFor \\( 86400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2 \\), the number of factors is:\n\n\\[\n(7 + 1)(3 + 1)(2 + 1) = 8 \\times 4 \\times 3 = 96\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion\nTherefore, the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, where \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers, is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{96}\n\\]",
